Below is a complete, practical setup for your exact rules using pipedog, plus a small custom Python script to handle the complex business rules that pipedog’s built‑in checks can’t express directly (e.g., “if promotion_description contains ‘%’ then percentage_discount must be positive”, “promotion_price < regular_price”, “no leading/trailing spaces”, etc.).
pipedog handles:
	- Unique, not‑null, numeric, min/max, allowed values, schema drift, row count, etc. out of the box.
	- For cross‑field and conditional logic, we’ll:
		Run pipedog for the basic checks.
		Run a small custom validator (Python + pandas) for the complex business rules.
	- Wrap both in a single daily script so your team just runs one command per file.

1) Prepare a sample file
_________________________

	- Create a representative sample of your scraped data, e.g. sample_scrape.csv (or .json), with columns: [unique_id, competitor_name, extraction_date, product_name, grammage_quantity, grammage_unit, regular_price, selling_price, price_valid_from, price_per_unit, percentage_discount, promotion_price, promotion_valid_from, promotion_valid_upto, promotion_description, currency, breadcrumb, pdp_url, region, pack_size, site_shown_uom]
	- Make sure this sample is clean (passes all your rules) – it will be used to create the baseline.
	
2) Create a profile for your dataset
____________________________________

	- Run:
	pipedog init sample_scrape.csv --profile product_scrape
	- This creates:
		.pipedog/product_scrape/schema.json – baseline schema & stats
		.pipedog/product_scrape/checks.json – auto‑generated rules
	- pipedog will auto‑generate rules like:
		not_null for columns with no nulls in the sample
		unique for columns where all values are distinct
		min_value / max_value for numeric columns
	- We’ll then customize checks.json to match your exact requirements.
	
3) Define your rules in pipedog
_______________________________
		
	- Open the checks file:
	pipedog checks edit --profile product_scrape	
	- Below is a recommended structure for your rules. Adjust column names/types to match your actual data.
	Eg checks.json :
	[
  		{
    			"column": "unique_id",
    			"type": "not_null",
    			"description": "unique_id must not be null",
			"severity": "error"
		},
		{
			"column": "unique_id",
			"type": "unique",
			"description": "unique_id must be unique",
			"severity": "error"
		},

		{
			"column": "pdp_url",
			"type": "not_null",
			"description": "pdp_url must not be null",
			"severity": "error"
		},
		...
